  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1: Chances for strong storms and flash flooding\ntoday and Monday.\n\nThe upper level flow pattern continues to remain unchanged today\ncompared to the last several days, with upper level ridging\ncentered across the central Gulf coast states, and an upper\nlevel trough across the Northeast. This has created a corridor\nfor shortwave vorticity maxima to travel from west to east from\nthe central plains into the Ohio valley, and into the Mid-\nAtlantic states. Today, high pressure that was over the\nNortheast creating some weak wedging across the Piedmont is\nexpected to lift north as a warm front pushes to the north and\neast by the early morning hours. This will transition winds to\nsouthwesterly/southerly areawide today. \n\nAt the surface, dewpoints will generally climb into the low 70s\nacross the entire region. This will allow for SBCAPE values to\nclimb into the 1500-2500 J/Kg level. DCAPE values in the\n900-1200 J/Kg range will support strong damaging winds with\nstorm clusters today. 0-6 Km deep layer shear looks to remain in\nthe 20-25 knot range, which will lead to the development of\nmulticellular clusters, with a few possible embedded supercell\nthunderstorms across the north central Piedmont of Virginia\ntoday. The aforementioned warm front may create pockets of low\nlevel directional shear in the 0-3 km level favorable enough to\ndevelop an isolated tornado across the central Virginia \nPiedmont. This corridor where clusters of storms look to track \nalong today may also develop training storms, which would lead \nto the possibility for flash flooding across the region. Current\nCAM guidance keeps much of the heaviest rainfall north of the \nregion; however, slight shifts south in the development of \nstorms may shift this threat further south into the region. \nCurrently, with largest threats for flash flooding look to be \nacross SE West Virginia, and portions of the I-64 corridor in \nVirginia. \n\nOverall the threat shifts south on Monday as a cold front\nfinally pushes through the region. This cold front will shift\nthe severe weather threat south towards the VA/NC border, with\nstrong to severe winds likely to be the major threat/impact to\nthe region. This front will also usher in a drier airmass by \nTuesday, which will be maintained across the region through the \nmiddle of the week. Rain chances don't look to return until \nThursday, with the passage of another cold front.\n\nSPC maintains Slight risks (level 2 of 5) for areas north of\n460 today, and for areas south of 460 for Monday. All other\nareas maintain a Marginal risk (level 1 of 5). WPC is\nmaintaining a Moderate risk (level 3 of 4) for portions of SE\nWest Virginia today, with a Slight risk (level 2 of 4) for \nareas north of 460, and a Marginal risk (level 1 of 4) for areas\nsouth of 460 for today. A Marginal risk (level 1 of 4) for areas\nwest of I-81 is forecast for Monday at this time.",
